1
00:00:23,926 --> 00:00:30,473
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:31,394 --> 00:00:39,082
We are currently discussing applications and system categories as part of lecture 2.

3
00:00:40,123 --> 00:00:41,905
Earlier we talked about VR applications.

4
00:00:42,966 --> 00:00:47,971
So, we will be continuing our discussion on that topic.

5
00:00:47,991 --> 00:00:52,836
So, we have so far covered applications related to VR and VR system categories.

6
00:00:53,508 --> 00:01:07,429
Now, let us continue our discussion and talk about applications related to AR and AR system categories.

7
00:01:07,449 --> 00:01:10,072
Just a quick recap of the use forecast.

8
00:01:10,132 --> 00:01:15,921
We have already seen this forecast many times and just to sum it up.

9
00:01:16,643 --> 00:01:23,917
There is huge potential for extended reality technologies in different application domains including video games,

10
00:01:23,917 --> 00:01:33,272
healthcare, engineering, live events, video entertainment, real estate, retail, military, and some more.

11
00:01:35,436 --> 00:01:41,166
And the statistic also talks about, apart from the percentage of

12
00:01:41,804 --> 00:01:52,399
systems in that domain, it can also give an indication of the kind of money involved even if the percentage may appear to be less.

13
00:01:54,422 --> 00:01:59,048
Overall, huge potential as we have repeatedly discussed.

14
00:01:59,889 --> 00:02:02,413
Now, let us quickly have a look at AR applications.

15
00:02:03,034 --> 00:02:08,300
The forecast that I have shown earlier is related to both VR and AR applications.

16
00:02:08,752 --> 00:02:15,101
VR applications we have discussed earlier, let us quickly have a look at the relevant AR applications.

17
00:02:17,884 --> 00:02:34,103
Now, the first thing that we should keep in mind is that while we were talking about VR, we emphasized the term immersive experience; in the case of AR, that is not mandatory. We do not

18
00:02:34,842 --> 00:02:44,293
pay much attention to the immersive experience; rather, we pay more attention to augmentation of real-world content with digital content.

19
00:02:46,635 --> 00:02:51,241
And such augmentations have many applications.

20
00:02:55,265 --> 00:02:57,488
We have AR applications in archaeology.

21
00:02:57,808 --> 00:03:03,955
Say, for example, if you look at this figure, you can see that there is something

22
00:03:04,492 --> 00:03:09,537
which is not visible with the naked eye, but we can add it on our screen.

23
00:03:10,218 --> 00:03:19,066
So reconstruction of lost structures can be a very interesting application area.

24
00:03:19,086 --> 00:03:30,417
Also in architecture, we can view the architecture, we can augment it with various information, and we can actually play around with the architecture through AR applications.

25
00:03:32,039 --> 00:03:33,300
Industrial manufacturing,

26
00:03:33,938 --> 00:03:37,401
We can have important applications.

27
00:03:37,441 --> 00:03:55,358
For example, in this figure, in this image, as you can see, somebody is getting to see things while repairing or trying to troubleshoot some component of an industrial setup through AR applications.

28
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:01,144
Commerce, particularly the e-commerce space, has a lot of AR applications,

29
00:04:03,773 --> 00:04:11,144
which is one of the motivational factors for this metaverse concept.

30
00:04:11,164 --> 00:04:15,010
Gaming, like VR, in AR also we can have a lot of gaming applications.

31
00:04:15,070 --> 00:04:17,452
One famous example is Pokemon Go.

32
00:04:18,495 --> 00:04:22,601
Medical, again like VR, we can have many medical applications.

33
00:04:23,022 --> 00:04:24,223
We will see later.

34
00:04:25,345 --> 00:04:27,348
And there can be many more such applications.

35
00:04:28,369 --> 00:04:33,477
Now let us have a quick look at a few interesting applications to give you an idea of

36
00:04:33,845 --> 00:04:35,987
what kind of things we are talking about here.

37
00:04:39,151 --> 00:04:47,560
Say one airport passenger app, Gatwick Airport, it was an old system of course.

38
00:04:48,461 --> 00:04:58,075
Now, what happened is that in that airport, 2000 plus beacons were placed at different points in the terminal, the passenger terminal,

39
00:04:58,075 --> 00:05:03,177
and through the app, the passenger can get to know where exactly he or she is and

40
00:05:03,848 --> 00:05:08,696
in which direction he or she needs to move to reach the desired terminal.

41
00:05:09,618 --> 00:05:15,667
For large terminals that are commonplace nowadays, these types of apps are very helpful.

42
00:05:20,776 --> 00:05:25,884
IKEA, a famous furniture company.

43
00:05:26,385 --> 00:05:29,350
Now, IKEA has got one app, the IKEA Place app.

44
00:05:30,713 --> 00:05:39,183
It allows you to scan your room and design the space by placing IKEA objects in the digital image of your room.

45
00:05:40,885 --> 00:05:47,133
That in turn can give you some idea of how your room will look if you buy the products.

46
00:05:47,153 --> 00:05:54,281
So, it is again a kind of customer experience before making a purchase, before buying something.

47
00:05:54,802 --> 00:05:56,704
So, you can actually test,

48
00:05:56,903 --> 00:06:07,414
the idea of purchasing particular pieces of furniture and placing them in your room and deciding whether to buy it depending on how it looks or whether you are satisfied.

49
00:06:13,139 --> 00:06:25,712
Now, Dulux also has a visualizer app similar in nature that is before you buy the paint, you can actually paint your walls digitally.

50
00:06:26,131 --> 00:06:41,975
on a screen and see the effect and based on that you can make your choice whether to go for buying the paint or not.

51
00:06:41,995 --> 00:06:54,433
We can have interesting other applications also say for example, we can have a tape measure to I mean an augmented reality application where we can use a virtual tape measure

52
00:06:55,662 --> 00:06:58,446
That can be used inside the room or outside the room.

53
00:06:58,546 --> 00:07:04,095
You do not need to carry a physical tape to measure heights of objects or lengths of objects.

54
00:07:04,635 --> 00:07:11,846
You can use your mobile for doing that by having this app installed on your device.

55
00:07:16,934 --> 00:07:22,362
Similar to this paint application or this IKEA furniture app,

56
00:07:23,742 --> 00:07:32,500
Similar other apps are also there, say for example, Sephora cosmetic company came up with this app idea that by using the AR technology,

57
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:51,488
a customer can try out different looks and depending on the level of satisfaction can decide to buy the product with the right color that satisfies the most.

58
00:07:54,978 --> 00:08:11,024
They can virtually try out those cosmetics on their digital appearance, digital face and different parts of the face to make an informed choice before actually purchasing the product.

59
00:08:11,364 --> 00:08:21,340
So these types of apps are very common in AR where different customer experiences are provided before making a purchase decision.

60
00:08:24,425 --> 00:08:34,918
A similar app is made by Rolex, where you can try out different watch models on your digital representation of your hand, that means your hand.

61
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:46,213
And then, depending on your level of satisfaction, whether you are satisfied with the look and feel, you can actually go for buying it.

62
00:08:54,344 --> 00:08:59,431
These are primarily from a customer experience point of view.

63
00:08:59,972 --> 00:09:21,280
In healthcare, there are many interesting applications that have been developed using AR from medical training to telemedicine options that enable medical professionals to interact with patients.

64
00:09:23,487 --> 00:09:34,403
An interesting application is to get real-time information to treatment areas to support diagnosis, surgery, and treatment plans.

65
00:09:34,623 --> 00:09:53,297
For example, AccuVein, a handheld device which has AR applications, can scan the vein network of a patient that leads to a 45% reduction in escalation.

66
00:09:53,297 --> 00:09:56,427
So, it can tell what the situation is, what the condition is.

67
00:09:57,569 --> 00:10:15,921
By pointing the device on the surface area of the skin, the internal vein network can be visualized by the user and then depending on that, the treatment course can be decided.

68
00:10:21,892 --> 00:10:25,196
So, those are some of the interesting applications.

69
00:10:25,617 --> 00:10:27,579
There are many more such interesting applications.

70
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:36,430
Whatever applications I have talked about, they came earlier, they came sometime back, but the modern applications are many more and

71
00:10:36,430 --> 00:10:47,813
many of the earlier applications have been revised, refined, and came up in modern avatar, which are better in performance as compared to the original versions.

72
00:10:47,813 --> 00:10:50,869
But as you can get some idea, so AR,

73
00:10:51,490 --> 00:10:56,697
can be used to basically come up with many interesting application areas.

74
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:06,208
Now, like VR, what we have done earlier in discussions related to VR, let us do similar things for AR.

75
00:11:06,789 --> 00:11:09,953
So, what kind of categories can we create for AR applications?

76
00:11:13,698 --> 00:11:17,042
Unlike in VR, where we primarily focus on,

77
00:11:18,153 --> 00:11:29,007
Psychological experience in AR since experience or immersive experience is not that important for categorization; we have to rely on the technology side.

78
00:11:29,027 --> 00:11:41,122
So, technologically, we can have broadly two categories: one is marker-based AR applications; the other one is markerless AR applications.

79
00:11:41,142 --> 00:11:42,844
Now, markerless AR application.

80
00:11:43,162 --> 00:11:48,988
can have four subcategories: location-based, superimposition, projection-based, and outlining-based.

81
00:11:49,369 --> 00:11:53,393
Let us have a quick look at each of these categories.

82
00:11:58,839 --> 00:12:01,021
Let us begin with marker-based.

83
00:12:02,443 --> 00:12:10,632
Now, as the name suggests, this type of application uses markers to trigger an augmented experience.

84
00:12:11,192 --> 00:12:12,814
That means there will be some sort of

85
00:12:13,165 --> 00:12:23,540
markers in the environment. When we point our device towards that marker, the digital content will be augmented on the screen view of the real world.

86
00:12:24,521 --> 00:12:38,481
For example, here you can see in this figure there is one QR code-like thing. When you are pointing your handheld device, like this tab here, towards that marker, you get to see

87
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:46,788
a digital augmentation, that is, this type of, say, some furniture at that spot.

88
00:12:47,089 --> 00:12:54,477
So you can basically check for yourself if you put furniture of this sort there, how it will look like.

89
00:12:55,058 --> 00:12:57,681
Now the furniture is placed where the marker is.

90
00:12:58,081 --> 00:13:04,608
So once your device detects the marker, then the desired augmentation is done at that point.

91
00:13:06,571 --> 00:13:08,653
So QR codes can be one such marker.

92
00:13:09,528 --> 00:13:16,576
There can be other markers like simple color codes, color bars, fiducial markers; many things can be there.

93
00:13:21,942 --> 00:13:27,888
Now, marker-based applications are easier to make, easier to build.

94
00:13:28,389 --> 00:13:30,832
However, they are difficult to maintain.

95
00:13:31,332 --> 00:13:34,936
Markers are prone to damage.

96
00:13:35,857 --> 00:13:38,320
So, once it is damaged, your app may not work.

97
00:13:39,818 --> 00:13:44,425
As opposed to these, we have marker-less applications.

98
00:13:45,286 --> 00:13:46,588
Now, these are more versatile.

99
00:13:46,608 --> 00:13:54,500
You do not need to place any marker anywhere in the physical world and things can be done based on computer vision techniques.

100
00:13:56,603 --> 00:14:02,892
So, marker-less applications are more versatile.

101
00:14:02,932 --> 00:14:06,197
They allow the users to decide where to put virtual objects.

102
00:14:08,945 --> 00:14:14,451
Now there are subcategories of such markerless applications.

103
00:14:15,072 --> 00:14:20,018
One of these subcategories is location based.

104
00:14:20,038 --> 00:14:37,078
Here the application relies on some sort of sensor, say for example GPS sensor, to detect the location of the user with respect to a reference frame and depending on a specific location.

105
00:14:37,565 --> 00:14:50,297
Digital content is augmented on the real world view of the scene and that location will be provided by the sensor, the location sensor.

106
00:14:50,938 --> 00:14:55,642
A common example is GPS based sensors, GPS sensors.

107
00:15:00,447 --> 00:15:06,593
Say for example, you are walking along a road with this view on your mobile and once you reach a specific

108
00:15:07,012 --> 00:15:14,420
Location reported by your GPS, something automatically appears. Say, for example, here, as you can see, this

109
00:15:14,420 --> 00:15:25,488
object appears on your screen, which is the digital augmentation of the real world view based on the location.

110
00:15:28,151 --> 00:15:31,213
Then we have superimposition based applications.

111
00:15:33,075 --> 00:15:35,517
So, in this type of AR application,

112
00:15:37,572 --> 00:15:54,526
the object in the physical world, one object or multiple objects, are recognized using computer vision techniques and then we can augment digital content depending on the recognized object.

113
00:15:54,566 --> 00:15:59,175
Say, for example, in this room setting we have detected, say, this table

114
00:16:00,758 --> 00:16:10,818
and some chairs, and based on that we can augment another chair here as shown in this small animation.

115
00:16:12,141 --> 00:16:14,004
So, this is superimposition based.

116
00:16:14,606 --> 00:16:19,976
So, we are superimposing digital content on a real world scene depending on recognition of objects.

117
00:16:21,439 --> 00:16:22,902
Then we have projection based

118
00:16:23,354 --> 00:16:26,258
where we use light projectors.

119
00:16:27,400 --> 00:16:38,215
Now, such projectors can project digital graphics, digital characters, digital content onto an object or surface.

120
00:16:38,235 --> 00:16:40,238
It can be a space also.

121
00:16:40,258 --> 00:16:47,488
Say, for example, holographic projection or it can be on a surface.

122
00:16:51,822 --> 00:16:57,107
Finally, we have outlining applications.

123
00:16:57,128 --> 00:17:02,813
These again use object recognition to recreate the user's immediate surroundings.

124
00:17:03,955 --> 00:17:16,867
So, for example, while driving in low light conditions, you can augment the view that the driver gets with other cars around which may not be

125
00:17:17,792 --> 00:17:20,377
visible to the driver due to low light conditions.

126
00:17:20,416 --> 00:17:28,968
You can digitally enhance the scene with the cars that are not clearly visible to the naked eye, as shown in this figure.

127
00:17:31,492 --> 00:17:39,483
So, that is another type of markerless augmented reality application.

128
00:17:40,044 --> 00:17:47,583
So, in summary, what we can say is that we learned about different applications.

129
00:17:47,583 --> 00:17:48,784
both in VR and AR.

130
00:17:49,685 --> 00:17:54,011
Also, we talked about system categories.

131
00:17:54,772 --> 00:17:58,436
For VR, experience is important; the psychological aspect is important.

132
00:17:58,877 --> 00:18:06,907
So, based on experience, we have talked about three categories of VR systems: non-immersive, semi-immersive, and fully immersive.

133
00:18:06,927 --> 00:18:10,311
Whereas, that type of psychological

134
00:18:10,949 --> 00:18:14,273
is not very important in AR.

135
00:18:14,853 --> 00:18:20,960
So here, when we talked about system categories, we primarily focused on technology.

136
00:18:22,982 --> 00:18:31,611
Depending on the technology used to develop and deploy the systems, we talked about broadly two categories: marker-based and markerless.

137
00:18:31,631 --> 00:18:33,092
Marker-based are easier to make.

138
00:18:34,274 --> 00:18:36,256
They rely on simple algorithms.

139
00:18:36,996 --> 00:18:37,497
However,

140
00:18:38,287 --> 00:18:45,455
Markers are difficult to maintain unless it is a very sanitized environment, say inside a museum.

141
00:18:47,518 --> 00:18:53,905
Well-attended environment is required to maintain the markers; otherwise, in outdoor settings, they are prone to damage.

142
00:18:54,186 --> 00:18:58,170
To overcome that, we can go for markerless techniques.

143
00:18:58,190 --> 00:19:00,293
Primarily, they rely on computer vision.

144
00:19:01,254 --> 00:19:07,281
They may rely on other sensors, like GPS sensors, in case of location-based applications.

145
00:19:08,543 --> 00:19:13,277
Computer vision-based applications include superimposition-based and outline-based.

146
00:19:13,277 --> 00:19:23,683
Whereas projection-based techniques, such as holographic projection, can be used in AR application development, as we have seen.

147
00:19:25,685 --> 00:19:37,200
So, these are broadly different system categories in AR and VR; we have also talked about different applications.

148
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:43,148
So, if you have noticed, the applications, more or less, broad domains are similar.

149
00:19:43,209 --> 00:19:50,138
However, in VR, we talk of immersive applications, whereas in AR, we do not put much emphasis on immersive applications.

150
00:19:50,198 --> 00:20:01,973
Instead, we focus more on other types of applications which rely on augmenting digital content onto the real-world view.

151
00:20:05,952 --> 00:20:09,416
In our next lecture, we will talk about some definitions.

152
00:20:09,436 --> 00:20:14,102
So far, we have mentioned terms in a very loose manner, in a very broad way.

153
00:20:14,122 --> 00:20:29,500
We will learn a more precise way of defining these terms, say VR, AR, MR, or other concepts like immersion, in the next lecture.

154
00:20:30,060 --> 00:20:33,985
Once those definitions are covered, it will be easier to discuss.

155
00:20:35,011 --> 00:20:43,786
in a more unambiguous manner, the relevant concepts and their applications.

156
00:20:43,806 --> 00:20:50,056
So, that is all we have to discuss under this topic.

157
00:20:50,076 --> 00:20:53,041
So, on applications and system categories.

158
00:20:53,281 --> 00:20:57,368
So, we have come to the end of our second lecture.

159
00:20:59,351 --> 00:21:00,293
I hope you have

160
00:21:01,707 --> 00:21:06,923
enjoyed the discussion and have learned the concepts I wanted to present.

161
00:21:08,347 --> 00:21:08,889
So, thank you.

162
00:21:09,270 --> 00:21:09,872
Happy learning.

163
00:21:10,313 --> 00:21:11,717
See you in the next lecture.
